'''''Alyssum serpyllifolium''''', the '''thyme-leaved alison''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Brassicaceae]], native to the western Mediterranean region.<ref name="RHS_26570">{{cite web |title=''Alyssum serpyllifolium'' thyme-leaved alison |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/26570/i-alyssum-serpyllifolium-i/details |date=2022 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=8 May 2022 |quote=1 suppliers |archive-date=13 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513091526/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/26570/i-alyssum-serpyllifolium-i/details |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="277782-1">{{cite POWO |id=277782-1 |title=''Alyssum serpyllifolium'' Desf. |access-date=8 May 2022}}</ref> It is adapted to [[serpentine soil]]s.<ref>{{cite journal |title=The Evolutionary Genomics of Serpentine Adaptation |year=2020 |last1=Konečná |first1=Veronika |last2=Yant |first2=Levi |last3=Kolář |first3=Filip |journal=Frontiers in Plant Science |volume=11 |article-number=574616 |doi=10.3389/fpls.2020.574616 |doi-access=free |pmid=33391295 |pmc=7772150}}</ref> The [[Royal Horticultural Society]] recommends it for [[rock gardens]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Rock gardens: plants |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/for-places/rock-gardens |date=2022 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=8 May 2022 |archive-date=21 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421060116/https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/for-places/rock-gardens |url-status=live}}</ref>

== Description ==
The flower color is yellow with green stems. It consists of lance-shaped leaves and requires dry soil and drained areas. This plant is often used to help observe the relationship between hyperaccumulating plants which store and absorb metals in their tissues, vs. non-hyperaccumulating plants.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last1=Ghaderian |first1=Y. S. Majid |last2=Lyon |first2=Anthony J. E. |last3=Baker |first3=Alan J. M. |date=2000 |title=Seedling Mortality of Metal Hyperaccumulator Plants Resulting from Damping off by ''Pythium'' spp |journal=The New Phytologist |volume=146 |issue=2 |pages=219–224 |doi=10.1046/j.1469-8137.2000.00645.x |jstor=2588971 |pmid=33862969 |bibcode=2000NewPh.146..219G }}</ref> If soil is contaminated with high concentrations of metals this is likely because of mineral rock weathering or as a result of industrialization.<ref name=":1" /> The discovery of the plant's abilities has led to an easier process of detoxification of plant soil caused by mineral rock weathering or industrialization.<ref name=":1" />

== Distribution ==
It is from the family [[Brassicaceae]] and is found in southwestern Europe. ''Alyssum serpyllifolium'' is mainly found in the Iberian Peninsula, Northeastern Portugal, and Spain.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last1=Sobczyk |first1=M. K. |last2=Smith |first2=J. a. C. |last3=Pollard |first3=A. J. |last4=Filatov |first4=D. A. |date=2016-10-16 |orig-date=26 October 2016 |title=Evolution of nickel hyperaccumulation and serpentine adaptation in the ''Alyssum serpyllifolium'' species complex |journal=Heredity |volume=118 |issue=1 |pages=31–41 |doi=10.1038/hdy.2016.93 |doi-access=free |pmid=27782119 |pmc=5176119}}</ref>

== Phytoremediation ==
One of the features that this plant developed was used to help it adapt to its soil which contains a high concentration of metals. It is considered to be a metal hyperaccumulator (plants that can tolerate high amounts of metal within their system).<ref>{{cite book |doi=10.1016/B978-0-12-817955-0.00002-8 |chapter=P-type ATPases and their role in metal homeostasis in plants |title=Metal and Nutrient Transporters in Abiotic Stress |date=2021 |last1=Keeran |first1=Nisha S. |last2=Usha |first2=Balasundaram |last3=Ganesan |first3=G. |pages=33–54 |isbn=978-0-12-817955-0 }}</ref> This plant was used in a phytoremediation experiment to absorb the metal contaminated soil.<ref name=":0" /> A form of phytoremediation is known as phytoextraction, which removes the metals from the contaminated soil by absorbing the metals through the roots.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Peuke |first1=Andreas D. |last2=Rennenberg |first2=Heinz |title=Phytoremediation: Molecular biology, requirements for application, environmental protection, public attention and feasibility |journal=EMBO Reports |date=June 2005 |volume=6 |issue=6 |pages=497–501 |doi=10.1038/sj.embor.7400445 |pmc=1369103 |pmid=15940279 }}</ref> It has the ability to uptake a high concentration of metals. This species of plant is a nickel hyperaccumulator, it mainly absorbs high levels of nickel because of the ultramafic rock found in its environment.<ref name=":0" />

